      "term": "Early Horizon",
      "matchText": "Early Horizon",
      "wikiSlug": "",
      "type": "concept",
      "definition": "The Early Horizon (roughly 900–200 BCE) is the first of Andean archaeology's three 'horizons' — periods when a single religious and artistic style spread across most of the central Andes. It is defined by the Chavín cult, whose fanged-deity imagery radiated out from the temple at Chavín de Huántar."
    },
    {
      "term": "Late Horizon",
      "matchText": "Late Horizon",
      "wikiSlug": "",
      "type": "concept",
      "definition": "The Late Horizon (1450–1532 CE) is the last of the three Andean 'horizons' — the brief, dazzling period when the Inca Empire imposed a single political and artistic order across the whole central Andes, ending with the Spanish conquest."
    },
